<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="AARCH64:LE:64:v8A:default">
<!--
    kuna: a register an AArch64 function sets for a system register is not
    the high word of its return; a prefetch or cache op on it is no such
    write.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 return recovery, the output trials of the
    function's own RETURN (ActionReturnRecovery, p4_calls/kuna_retsysreg.rs).
    P0 assertion: option retsysreg on|off (default on).

    Source (clang -target aarch64-linux-gnu -O2 at 0x100000, bytes verbatim):

        long pmr(long a) { long r = a * 3; register long v asm("x1") = 0xf0;
          __asm volatile("msr s3_0_c4_c6_0, %0" :: "r"(v)); return r; }
        fpcr, daif: the same with msr fpcr (0x3000000) and msr daif (0x3c0)
        struct pr { long a, b; };
        struct pr two(long *p, long n) { long b = p[n];
          __builtin_prefetch((void*)b); return (struct pr){p[0], b}; }
        __int128 i128(long *p) { long hi = p[1];
          __builtin_prefetch((void*)hi, 1, 3);
          return ((__int128)hi << 64) | (unsigned long)p[0]; }
        cvau, civac, tlbi, ic: two with dc cvau, dc civac, tlbi vae1 and
          ic ivau on b

    pmr is add x0,x0,x0,lsl #1; mov w1,#0xf0; msr ICC_PMR_EL1,x1; ret.

    The bug: x1 joined x0 as a 16-byte return whose high word is the
    system register's operand.  The fix returns the 64-bit value from pmr,
    fpcr and daif.  two, i128, cvau, civac, tlbi and ic keep the pair: a
    prefetch, cache or TLB op takes an address, which the high word can be,
    and sets no processor state.  The last pass sets option retsysreg off
    and prints pmr and fpcr with the pair again.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
0004008b011e8052014618d5c0035fd60004008b0160a05201441bd5c0035fd6
0004008b0178805221421bd5c0035fd6017861f8200080f9000040f9c0035fd6
000440a9300080f9c0035fd6017861f8217b0bd5000040f9c0035fd6017861f8
217e0bd5000040f9c0035fd6017861f8218708d5000040f9c0035fd6017861f8
21750bd5000040f9c0035fd6
</bytechunk>
